quality holds. So for |p+q| ≥ 2, the bound is exactly floor((k+1)/2). For |p+q| = 1, they treat separately and claim the bound still holds. Let's verify that for |p+q| = 1, the bound is also ≤ floor((k+1)/2). We need to show that the number of points is ≤ floor((k+1)/2). They argue that q ≥ 2 (or p ≥ 2) and then bound number of t by floor(k/q) + 1 ≤ floor((k+1)/2). Let's verify: Since q ≥ 2, floor(k/q) ≤ floor(k/2). So floor(k/q) + 1 ≤ floor(k/2) + 1. For k even, k=2m, floor(k/2) + 1 = m + 1 = floor((2m+1)/2) = floor((k+1)/2). For k odd, k=2m+1, floor(k/2) + 1 = m + 1 = floor((2m+2)/2) = floor((k+1)/2). So indeed floor(k/q) + 1 ≤ floor((k+1)/2). So bound holds.\n\nThus the bound is correct.\n\nNow they claim that the bound is sharp, and that lines achieving the bound must have |p+q| = 2. Let's verify: For a line to have exactly floor((k+1)/2) points in T_k, we need |p+q| = 2. Because if |p+q| ≥ 3, then floor((k-1)/|p+q|) + 1 ≤ floor((k-1)/3) + 1 ≤ floor((k+1)/2) - 1 for k≥? Let's check: For k≥3, floor((k-1)/3) + 1 ≤ floor((k+1)/2) - 1? Not always, but we can check. For k=3, floor((2)/3) + 1 = 0 + 1 = 1, floor((4)/2) = 2, so less. For k=5, floor(4/3)+1 = 1+1=2, floor(6/2)=3, less. So indeed less. So only |p+q| = 2 can achieve the bound. For |p+q| = 1, we also get bound ≤ floor((k+1)/2), but can we achieve equality? Let's test with k odd, say k=3, floor((k+1)/2) = 2. For p+q = 1, can we have a line covering 2 points? Yes, we can have line x + 2y = 5 (p=-1,q=2) which has |p+q| = 1? Wait p+q = -1+2 = 1, yes. That line is one of the three lines they list: y = -1/2 x + 5/2. That line has p = -1, q = 2, p+q = 1. So it has |p+q| = 1, not 2. Wait they said lines with |p+q| = 2 give three possibilities: p=q=1 (p+q=2), p=-2,q=1 (p+q=-1), p=-1,q=2 (p+q=1). So indeed they include p+q = ±1 as well. So they consider |p+q| = 2? Actually they said \"Equation (2.1) with |p+q|=2 gives three possibilities: p=q=1 (slope 1); p=-2,q=1 (slope -2); p=-1,q=2 (slope -1/2)\". Wait p+q for p=-2